       "Quality is written by John Galsworthy (1867–1933), the famous English novelist and playwright.",
       "Galsworthy received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1932; his famous work is The Forsyte Saga.",
       "The story revolves around the bootmaking shop of the Gessler brothers in a small street in London's West End.",
       "The Gessler brothers were German immigrants skilled in the art of bootmaking.",
       "The narrator had been their customer since childhood; his father too got boots made by them.",
       "The shop had no signboard — only a few pairs of boots were on display.",
       "The Gessler brothers made boots only on advance order, and their boots never failed to fit.",
       "Their boots were remarkable for lasting an exceptionally long time.",
       "When the narrator asked if bootmaking was very hard, Gessler smiled and said — Id is an ardt (It is an art).",
       "Once the narrator ordered Russian leather boots; Gessler showed him a piece of gold-brown leather and promised delivery in a fortnight.",
       "When the narrator complained that his last pair creaked, Gessler was shocked and offered to repair or replace them.",
       "Once the narrator came wearing boots bought from a large firm; Gessler at once knew they were not his and pointed out where the left boot pinched.",
       "Gessler bitterly said that big firms made poor boots and spoke of the hard times of his own trade.",
       "Moved, the narrator ordered many pairs of boots.",
       "Two years later, when the narrator returned to the shop, he learnt that the elder brother had died.",
       "Next time, half the shop was occupied by another company, and Gessler looked rapidly aged and frail.",
       "Finally the narrator learnt that Gessler had died of slow starvation — as the doctor called it.",
       "The new owner of the shop told that Gessler never advertised, used only the best leather and did all the work himself.",
       "Gessler spent all his earnings on rent and leather; he saved nothing for himself.",
       "The main theme of the story is craftsmanship versus commercialism — devotion to quality against cheap, advertised products.",
